<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Reichenau</h1><p class="st-meta">724 CE – 1540 CE (approx.) · capital Reichenau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An island abbey on Lake Constance and one of the great centres of Carolingian and Ottonian learning and book painting, now a World Heritage Site.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 724 the wandering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pirmin">Pirmin</a> founded a Benedictine monastery on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichenau-island">Reichenau</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>, with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>. It quickly became one of the leading abbeys of the Frankish realm, receiving estates across Alamannia and close links with the Carolingian court.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichenau-island">Reichenau</a> flourished in the 9th to 11th centuries: Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walafrid-strabo">Walafrid Strabo</a> wrote his poem on gardening, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hortulus">Hortulus</a>, there; the abbey library was among the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; and its scriptorium produced Ottonian illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> for emperors. Its abbots were imperial princes who ruled a small territory around the lake.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The abbey declined from the 13th century as its income fell and its noble-only admissions shrank the community. In 1540 it was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-constance">Prince-Bishopric of Constance</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> became its abbots. It was secularised in 1803; its churches at Oberzell, Mittelzell and Niederzell survive.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-reichenau.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Reichenau" width="320" height="186"><figcaption>Image: Hilarmont · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kloster_Reichenau_(Foto_Hilarmont).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>724 CE – 1540 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Reichenau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pirmin, Walafrid Strabo, Berno of Reichenau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Old and Middle High German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Reichenau Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1132&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-reichenau" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Reichenau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4891 506 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Reichenau lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5211 762L5229 694M5211 762L5216 762"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5216 762h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5211 762h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5211 762h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1132&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-reichenau">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
